Corrected the driver example in the documentation. It declared `id` and `normalize`, neither of which is a name on `IDriver`, so following it produced a driver the registry would accept and nothing would ever call. The example now subclasses `BaseDriver` and uses the real names — `driver_id`, `title`, `config_schema`, `subject`, `normalize_claims` — and says that a driver lives in its own module. @ericof
